WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.312
FastAPI makes request modeling convenient,

2
00:00:05.392 --> 00:00:10.203
but request data still deserves
skepticism.

3
00:00:10.283 --> 00:00:18.387
Inputs may come from path parameters,
query parameters, headers, cookies,

4
00:00:18.467 --> 00:00:22.519
JSON bodies, forms, and file uploads.

5
00:00:22.599 --> 00:00:30.450
They may be sent by browsers, mobile
applications, command line clients,

6
00:00:30.530 --> 00:00:37.747
partner systems, automation, or services
inside the environment.

7
00:00:37.827 --> 00:00:45.931
The defensive default is to treat request
data as untrusted until it has been

8
00:00:46.011 --> 00:00:49.937
validated for the specific use case.

9
00:00:50.017 --> 00:00:58.754
Type validation is valuable because it
catches malformed data early and makes API

10
00:00:58.834 --> 00:01:00.986
contracts clearer.

11
00:01:01.066 --> 00:01:03.852
It is not enough by itself.

12
00:01:03.932 --> 00:01:09.883
Correctly typed data can still violate
business rules,

13
00:01:09.963 --> 00:01:17.561
authorization rules, ownership boundaries,
limits, workflow state,

14
00:01:17.641 --> 00:01:19.920
or tenant isolation.

15
00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:25.571
A valid integer may identify someone
else's record.

16
00:01:25.651 --> 00:01:30.210
A valid string may be too broad for a
search.

17
00:01:30.290 --> 00:01:39.153
A valid file may still exceed size,
content, storage, or processing expectations.

18
00:01:39.233 --> 00:01:47.337
Request models and schema validation
should describe what the API accepts,

19
00:01:47.417 --> 00:01:56.407
including required fields, optional
fields, formats, ranges, and allowed values.

20
00:01:56.487 --> 00:02:04.971
Unexpected fields should be handled
deliberately so clients cannot influence

21
00:02:05.051 --> 00:02:09.863
server-controlled attributes by accident.

22
00:02:09.943 --> 00:02:16.401
Size limits matter because request bodies,
uploaded files,

23
00:02:16.481 --> 00:02:23.192
and nested objects can consume memory,
storage, parsing time,

24
00:02:23.272 --> 00:02:28.463
and downstream capacity if they are not
bounded.

25
00:02:28.543 --> 00:02:33.355
Safe error responses are part of
validation.

26
00:02:33.435 --> 00:02:39.639
Clients need enough information to correct
bad requests,

27
00:02:39.719 --> 00:02:47.317
but they do not need raw stack traces,
internal model details, secrets,

28
00:02:47.397 --> 00:02:51.575
query fragments, or service topology.

29
00:02:51.655 --> 00:02:59.000
Clear validation boundaries make path
operations easier to review:

30
00:02:59.080 --> 00:03:05.157
parse the request, validate shape and
business meaning,

31
00:03:05.237 --> 00:03:12.961
authenticate the caller, authorize the
action, perform the operation,

32
00:03:13.041 --> 00:03:17.220
and return only the intended response.
